---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Ornament from '../../components/Ornament.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Albion City Bonus & Return Rates Explained"
  description="Which Albion city refines what, how the return-rate formula works (15.25% base, 36.7% bonus city, 53.9% with focus) and what it saves in silver."
  canonical="/guides/refining-city-bonus/"
  keywords={[
    'albion city bonus',
    'albion refining bonus city',
    'albion resource return rate',
    'albion which city refines what',
    'albion refining guide',
  ]}
  jsonLd={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'How the Albion city bonus works - refining and crafting return rates',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'AlbionForge' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'AlbionForge' },
    datePublished: '2026-10-11',
    url: 'https://albionforge.top/guides/refining-city-bonus/',
  }}
>
  <div class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> › City bonus</div>
  <article class="section prose">
    <h1>How the City Bonus Works</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The single biggest lever on refining and crafting profit in Albion Online is simply being in
      the right city. Here is exactly what the bonus does, and what it is worth.
    </p>

    <h2>Every royal city specialises in one resource</h2>
    <p>
      Each of the five royal cities gives a boosted resource return rate when you
      <strong>refine the resource of its surrounding biome</strong>. Refine that resource anywhere
      else and you only get the base return rate. The bonus applies automatically at the city's
      refineries - there is nothing to activate.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>City</th><th>Biome</th><th>Bonus resource</th><th class="num">Return rate</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Thetford</td><td>Swamp</td><td>Ore → Metal Bars</td><td class="num">36.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fort Sterling</td><td>Mountain / Snow</td><td>Wood → Planks</td><td class="num">36.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Martlock</td><td>Highland</td><td>Hide → Leather</td><td class="num">36.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lymhurst</td><td>Forest</td><td>Fiber → Cloth</td><td class="num">36.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bridgewatch</td><td>Steppe / Desert</td><td>Rock → Stone Blocks</td><td class="num">36.7%</td></tr>
          <tr class="total-row"><td>Any other location</td><td>-</td><td>No refining bonus</td><td class="num">0–15.25%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The return rate formula</h2>
    <p>
      Return rates are not arbitrary numbers - they come from one formula. The game adds up
      <em>production bonus points</em> from every source that applies, then converts them into a
      return rate:
    </p>
    <div class="formula">
      return&nbsp;rate&nbsp;=&nbsp;bonus&nbsp;÷&nbsp;(100&nbsp;+&nbsp;bonus)
    </div>
    <p>The bonus points stack additively:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Source</th><th class="num">Bonus points</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Any royal city (base)</td><td class="num">+18</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Refining the city's specialised resource</td><td class="num">+40</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Crafting the city's specialised lines</td><td class="num">+15</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Using focus</td><td class="num">+59</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Which produces the familiar numbers:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Situation</th><th class="num">Bonus</th><th class="num">Return rate</th><th class="num">Effective input cost</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Royal city, no bonus, no focus</td><td class="num">18</td><td class="num">15.25%</td><td class="num">84.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Non-bonus city + focus</td><td class="num">77</td><td class="num">43.50%</td><td class="num">56.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Refining bonus city</td><td class="num">58</td><td class="num">36.71%</td><td class="num">63.3%</td></tr>
          <tr class="total-row"><td>Refining bonus city + focus</td><td class="num">117</td><td class="num">53.92%</td><td class="num">46.1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Crafting bonus city</td><td class="num">33</td><td class="num">24.81%</td><td class="num">75.2%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Crafting bonus city + focus</td><td class="num">92</td><td class="num">47.92%</td><td class="num">52.1%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cite">
      Formula and bonus values per
      <a href="https://albionfreemarket.com/articles/view/how-resource-return-rate-is-calculated-in-albion-online" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Albion Free Market's return rate analysis</a>;
      city specialisations per the <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/2022/https://wiki.albiononline.com/wiki/Refining" rel="noopener" target="_blank">official Albion wiki (archived)</a>.
      Player islands do not receive the royal city base bonus. Values last verified October 2026.
    </p>

    <h2>What that means in silver</h2>
    <p>
      At a 36.7&nbsp;% return rate you effectively pay <strong>63.3&nbsp;%</strong> of the recipe's
      listed material cost. On a Tier&nbsp;8 refine that routinely saves hundreds of thousands of
      silver per full stack - which is why experienced refiners haul raw material <em>to</em> the
      bonus city rather than refining where they gathered. The
      <a href="/tools/refining/">refining calculator</a> lets you price both sides of that decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      For crafting, note the bonus is smaller (+15, giving 24.8&nbsp;%), but it applies to
      <em>every input</em> - including artifacts and runes/souls/relics on enchanted recipes - so
      bonus-city crafting of artifact gear is standard practice. Price it in the
      <a href="/tools/crafting/">crafting calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <Ornament />

    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Refining on an island</strong> - no royal base bonus (and no specialisation bonus). Convenient, but you pay full recipe price.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignoring the return's value</strong> - returns arrive as materials, not silver. They only become profit once you re-use or sell them; the calculator's “sell returns” mode prices that honestly.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting specialization affects focus, not returns</strong> - your return rate is fixed by location and focus; higher spec only cuts focus cost per craft.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Next: <a href="/guides/focus-worth-it/">is focus worth using?</a> or
      <a href="/guides/market-tax/">how market tax eats your margin</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
